content::
List: Ordered, changeable, duplicates allowed, indexing. Square brackets.
Tuple: Ordered, unchangeable, duplicates allowed, indexing. Parentheses.
Set: Unique values, no indexing, changeable. Curly brackets.
Dictionary: Key value pairs, unique keys, ordered, insertion order, changeable. Curly brackets.
An empty set is set Parentheses, while Curly brackets is an empty dictionary.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "python.1 .list tuple set Dictionary.mp3"

content::
A Python virtual environment. v e n v.
is an isolated environment for a Python project. 
It lets each project have its own libraries and versions without affecting other Python projects.
step 1. Create a virtual environment.
go to your project folder.
use command.
python 3. space. minus m. space .v e n v. space. dot v e n v.
explain.
python 3 means Python interpreter.
minus m space .v e n v. means. Runs Python's built-in virtual environment module.
dot v e n v. means The folder where the environment is created.
step 2. Activate the virtual environment.
for linux, we can use.
source. space. dot v e n v. forward slash. bin. forward slash. activate.
for windows, we can use.
dot v e n v. backward slash. Scripts. backward slash. Activate dot ps1.
After activating, install packages using. p i p.
These packages are installed inside dot v e n v. not globally.
Python will use the interpreter and libraries from your active virtual environment.
When you finish working, use Deactivate command to Deactivate the environment.
You can save the installed libraries and their versions in requirements dot txt.
Therefore, you can install them later in another environment.
Add dot v e n v folder to gitignore so you don't upload your local virtual environment to GitHub.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "python.2 .virtual environment.mp3"

content::
Python often uses double underscores in special method names, such as init, len, s t r, add, l t, d e l. 
These are called special methods or dunder methods (short for double underscore).
They allow Python objects to work with built-in operations, such as creating objects, printing, adding, and comparing.
init is a special method called an initializer. 
It is automatically called when you create a new object. 
Its main purpose is to initialize the object's instance variables.
It is similar to a constructor in Java.
self refers to the current object.
you don't need to declare instance variables separately before using them. You can create them directly using self inside a method, commonly in __init__().
